course: dictionary
lesson: 15319
title: Smoothness: two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Smoothness is a noun. It names the quality of being smooth, and it has two main meanings. The first is being flat and even, with no bumps or rough parts: «The smoothness of the marble table made it lovely to touch.»
explain: The second meaning is being calm and easy, without problems: «The smoothness of our trip surprised us, because nothing went wrong.» Ask yourself whether the thing is flat or rough, or whether things are going well or badly. That tells you which meaning fits.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in a word you already know. In «the smoothness of the table», you can say «the flatness of the table» and it still makes sense, so the first meaning is right.
fact: Smoothness is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say «the smoothness» or «some smoothness», not «a smoothness».
fact: «Smoothness» means being flat and even, with no bumps or rough parts.
fact: «Smoothness» can also mean being calm and easy, without problems.
fact: In science, smooth surfaces have less friction, so things slide over them more easily.
fact: You can talk about the smoothness of skin, paint, or a car ride.
exercise: «The smoothness of the glass made the marble roll very fast.» What does smoothness mean here? | *Flat and even, with no bumps | Very loud and noisy | Bright and sparkly in the sun | Being sad and tired
exercise: «The smoothness of the ice let the puck slide a long way.» What does this tell you about the ice? | It was full of holes and cracks | *It was flat and even, with no rough parts | It was warm and wet | It was very old and dusty
exercise: Dad says, «The smoothness of our drive to the coast was lovely, with no traffic or problems.» What does smoothness mean here? | Cold and frosty | Very bumpy and rough | Noisy and slow | *Calm and easy, without problems
test: What does smoothness mean in «the smoothness of her new paint»? | Hot and sticky | *Flat and even, with no rough bits | Very loud and noisy | Lost and confused
test: «The smoothness of the week was a surprise, with no arguments and no trouble.» What does smoothness mean here? | Cold and wet | *Calm and easy, without problems | Very tall | Rough and bumpy
exam: Why do scientists compare the smoothness of surfaces? | *To test friction, because smooth surfaces have less friction | To measure how heavy a surface is | To find out how old a surface is | To count the bumps so they can add more
